# Day 8 - Git & GitHub Basics

## Commands Learned

git init
→ Initialize a new Git repository

git clone <repo_url>
→ Clone a remote repository

git status
→ Check current changes

git add <file>
→ Add file to staging area

git commit -m "message"
→ Save changes with a message

git push
→ Upload changes to GitHub

git pull
→ Fetch and merge latest changes from remote

git log
→ View commit history

## Branching Commands

git branch
→ List branches

git branch <branch_name>
→ Create new branch

git checkout <branch_name>
→ Switch branch

git checkout -b <branch_name>
→ Create and switch branch

git merge <branch_name>
→ Merge branch into current branch

## Key Concepts

Repository = Project folder tracked by Git

Commit = Snapshot of changes

Branch = Separate version of code

Main branch = Primary working branch

Merge = Combine changes from different branches

## Workflow

1. Make changes in files
2. git add <file>
3. git commit -m "message"
4. git push

→ Changes saved locally and uploaded to GitHub

## Branching Workflow

* Create new branch for feature
* Work on branch
* Merge into main after completion

Example:

git checkout -b feature1
→ work
git add .
git commit -m "feature added"
git checkout main
git merge feature1

## Observations

* Git tracks changes over time
* Branching helps work safely without breaking main code
* Commit messages should be meaningful
* Multiple developers can work using branches

## Debugging Learned

* If changes not showing → check git status
* If commit failed → check message
* If push failed → check remote connection
* If merge conflict → resolve manually

## What I Understood

* Git is essential for version control
* Branching allows safe development
* GitHub stores and shares code
* Proper workflow is important in teams

## Real Life Use

* Teams collaborate using Git
* Code changes are tracked and reviewed
* CI/CD pipelines trigger on git push
* Used in every software and DevOps workflow
